1335c4067dc4142f74#1"},{"title":"Metal oxide coated lithium cobalt fluorophosphate cathode materials for lithium secondary batteries--effect of aging and temperature - PubMed - . 2014 Oct;14(10):7545-52.\ndoi: 10.1166/jnn.2014.9561. \n\n# Metal oxide coated lithium cobalt fluorophosphate cathode materials f","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25942823/#1"},{"title":"氟代聚阴离子型正极材料的高电压储锂性能优化研究-手机知网","url":"https://wap.cnki.net/touch/web/Dissertation/Article/1021155126.nh.html"},{"title":"Fluoride phosphate Li2CoPO4F as a high-voltage cathode in Li-ion batteries","url":"https://fub-hagen.digibib.net/search/eds/record/edscal:edscal.17169661?be-eds-facetfilter=1%2CJournal%3Ajournal+of+power+sources&be-eds-facetfilter=2%2CSubjectEDS%3Aelectrical+engineering.+electrical+power+engineering&be-eds-sort=relevance&count=20&q-ky=%22High+voltage%22&start=1&pos=9"},{"title":"Fluoride phosphate Li2CoPO4F as a high-voltage cathode in Li-ion batteries - Skip to main contentSkip to article","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0378775305004350#1"}]  # Li₂CoPO₄F: A 5 V Lithium Cobalt Fluorophosphate Cathode Material  ## Overview Li₂CoPO₄F (lithium cobalt fluorophosphate) is a promising **5 V-class high-voltage cathode material** for lithium secondary batteries, identified as a candidate for next-generation post-4 V cathodes. Its high operating voltage stems from the combination of polyanionic inductive effects and fluorine's strong electronegativity, which raises the redox potential compared to oxide cathodes.  ## Key Electrochemical Properties - **Average potential:** ~5.1 V vs. Li⁺/Li⁰ (operating potentials up to 5.2 V) - **Theoretical specific capacity:** 143 mAh/g with an energy density of ~730 Wh/kg - **Structure:** Orthorhombic, Pnma space group (confirmed by X-ray and neutron diffraction) - **Synthesis:** Typically prepared by solid-state reaction (e.g., heating LiCoPO₄ with LiF at ~780 °C in a vacuum quartz tube)  ## Practical Performance and Challenges The material suffers from **electrolyte oxidation at high operating voltages** and metal ion dissolution, which limit capacity retention and cyclability. The true capacity and cyclability were initially difficult to assess due to the lack of suitable liquid electrolytes stable at 5 V.  ### Surface Coating Improvements - **Al₂O₃ coating (~3 nm amorphous layer):** Achieved an initial discharge capacity of ~127 mAh/g between 2 and 5.1 V, and improved capacity retention at C/2 rate from 53% (pristine) to 73% (1 wt% Al₂O₃-coated), by protecting the surface during high-voltage operation and reducing electrolyte oxidation/irreversible capacity loss. - **ZrO₂ coating:** Improved electrochemical activity with a **maximum discharge capacity of 144 mAh/g**, reduced metal ion dissolution to 1/8 of the uncoated material, and was crucial for stable performance up to 5.2 V. Elevated-temperature aging (30 days at 50 °C) did not degrade the intrinsic properties of the coated material, though impedance increased due to thick SEI layer formation.  ## Comparison and Significance Li₂CoPO₄F has a **theoretical capacity twice that of LiCoPO₄** (olivine, ~4.8 V plateau) and exhibits a slightly higher open-circuit voltage. However, commercialization faces challenges including metal dissolution and oxygen release at elevated temperatures, which surface coatings help mitigate.   REMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.
