[tool_result]Web search results for query: "LiCoMnO4 spinel cathode 5 V average voltage lithium"  Links: [{"title":"Delithiation/relithiation process of LiCoMnO4 spinel as 5 V electrode material - Journal of Power Sources","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0378775317313824?via%3Dihub#1"},{"title":"Towards an All Solid State Lithium Battery Beyond 5 V - The following article is Free article","url":"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1149/MA2016-03/2/961#1"},{"title":"Delithiation/relithiation process of LiCoMnO4 spinel as 5 V electrode material","url":"https://www.infona.pl/resource/bwmeta1.element.elsevier-3a366f80-86f5-3ee8-93a2-c798a4a26792"},{"title":"Single crystal synthesis, crystal structure and electrochemical property of spinel-type LiCoMnO4 as 5 V positive electrode materials","url":"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/jcersj2/124/6/124_16020/_article/-char/ja"},{"title":"Electrochemical Property of LiCoMnO4 Cathode Thin Film Prepared By PLD and Oxygen Post Annealing - The following article is Free article","url":"https://beta.iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1149/MA2016-02/3/393/meta#1"},{"title":"Towards an All Solid State Lithium Battery Beyond 5 V - Skip to Content","url":"https://katalog.slub-dresden.de/en/id/ai-49-aHR0cDovL2R4LmRvaS5vcmcvMTAuMTE0OS9tYTIwMTYtMDMvMi85NjE#1"},{"title":"Article Catalogues (Tables of Contents reviews)","url":"https://serials.unibo.it/cgi-ser/start/en/spogli/df-s.tcl?prog_art=6097043&language=ENGLISH&view=articoli"},{"title":"Delithiation/relithiation process of LiCoMnO4 spinel as 5 V electrode material","url":"https://www.cheric.org/research/tech/periodicals/view.php?seq=1602628"},{"title":"A novel synthetic route of micrometer-sized LiCoMnO4 as 5 V cathode material for advanced lithium ion batteries - Skip to main contentSkip to article","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167273818309615#1"},{"title":"High dimensional stability of LiCoMnO4 as positive electrodes operating at high voltage for lithium-ion batteries with a long cycle life - Electrochimica Acta","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0013468617326245?via%3Dihub#1"}]  # Summary: LiCoMnO₄ Spinel Cathode — 5 V Average Voltage Lithium  LiCoMnO₄ is a cubic spinel-structured cathode material (space group *Fd-3m*, lattice parameter a ≈ 8.058 Å) that operates at an average voltage of approximately **5 V vs. Li⁺/Li**, making it a leading candidate for high-voltage, high-energy-density lithium-ion batteries.  ## Key Electrochemical Characteristics  - **Average operating voltage**: ~5.0 V vs. Li⁺/Li, with discharge plateaus observed at **5.1 V and 4.9 V** corresponding to the Co³⁺/Co⁴⁺ redox couple, plus a minor plateau at 3.9 V associated with the Mn³⁺/Mn⁴⁺ redox couple. - **Discharge capacity**: Reported values range from ~95 mAh g⁻¹ (Kawai et al., long plateau centered on 5.0 V) to 105–107 mAh g⁻¹ (micrometer-sized and single-crystal samples) and up to **120 mAh g⁻¹** for well-crystallized material prepared by a two-step solid-state reaction. - **Energy density**: Theoretical discharge energy density is ~740 Wh/kg, approximately **1.3× higher than LiCoO₂** (a 25% increase). - **Full delithiation** requires potentials up to 5.6 V; however, only about **70% of lithium** can be extracted/reinserted electrochemically in a practical 4.5–5.4 V window limited by electrolyte stability.  ## Structural Stability and Cycling  - Lithium extraction/insertion proceeds via a **single-phase (solid-solution) mechanism** across the full lithium content range. - The material exhibits **high dimensional stability**: lattice parameter changes of only ~0.7% (one-third of typical spinel-framework oxides) and electrode thickness changes of ~0.5%, enabling long cycle life (electrochemical performance maintained over 100 cycles). - LiCoMnO₄ shows good cycling performance in thin-film all-solid-state battery configurations.  ## Challenges  - **Electrolyte instability**: Common liquid electrolytes decompose above ~4.5 V, restricting capacity; all-solid-state approaches with solid electrolytes are being pursued to access the full ~5.3 V potential. - **4 V parasitic reactions**: Oxygen loss during synthesis creates Mn³⁺ sites leading to an unwanted ~4 V peak; controlling oxygen content (e.g., post-annealing at 600 °C for 6 h in O₂) suppresses this and maximizes 5 V capacity. - **Synthesis considerations**: High-temperature processing can cause lithium/oxygen loss; two-step solid-state reactions, sol-gel methods, and soft-chemical routes from layered precursors have been developed to obtain well-crystallized or micrometer-sized particles with improved performance.   REMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.
