 all other cell choices fixed. 20	 21	Contract criteria: 22	- (A) Envelope layer: candidate's computed point (avg voltage, capacity, relative stability) must lie OUTSIDE the pre-registered 84-compound documented-cathode convex hull (runs/exp/known_set_comp/envelope_stats_comp.json; adjudicated by comp_envelope_check.py, Delaunay outside test, replayable). 23	- (B) Property window: computed average voltage >= 5.3 V; run-comp machinery (CHGNet; layered/olivine prototype dispatch; other prototypes disclosed as domain limits) must converge-energy per screening caliber (converged=false accepted at screening precision). 24	- (B') True-voltage guard: true (QE-level or literature-consistent) voltage >= 4.95 V; computed-only high values from Ni-olivine overestimate family rejected. 25	- (C) Bridge rule: constant OCP = computed average voltage; capacity scaled from computed capacity x 0.90; SEI kinetics = baseline k0; ED_active = V x C x 0.9 reported. 26	- (D) Closed-loop adjudication: finalist re-computed independently (run-comp replay + comp_envelope_check.py) + run-qe (QE voltage with Li-metal reference) + Materials-Project/catalog check; verdict records "not found in searched scope". 27	 28	GUIDANCE: documented cathodes don't reach this window under true-voltage measure; the practical high-voltage family (Ni-olivines) computes inflated values treated as unreliable. Attaining the window requires leaving the documented cathode chemistry system — e.g., importing chemistry from outside the cathode domain (fluorophosphates, oxyfluorides, other frameworks) and computing it honestly. 29	 30	First steps: explore the workspace to understand the tools. Let me look at the directory structure, the runs/exp folder, known_set_comp, the scripts (run-comp, comp_envelope_check.py, run-qe), and prior experiment runs (t9new_r1 might be a run directory with previous artifacts). 31	 32	Let me start by exploring the workspace structure. 33	 34	I should: 35	1. Explore the workspace and repo structure. 36	2. Read the pre-registered envelope stats JSON. 37	3. Read the scripts: run-comp, comp_envelope_check.py, run-qe. 38	4. Understand the screening machinery (CHGNet; layered/olivine prototype dispatch). 39	5. Find the Chen2020 NMC811 profile config. 40	6. Check prior runs (t9new_r1 might have prior attempts). 41	7. Propose candidate compositions: need computed avg voltage >= 5.3 V, true voltage >= 4.95 V, outside the documented hull. 42	 43	Known chemistry ideas for very high voltage cathodes with literature-consistent true voltages ≥ 4.95 V: 44	- LiCoPO4 olivine: ~4.8 V (literature) — CHGNet might compute it around 4.8-4.9. Hmm, close but maybe not ≥ 4.95. 45	- LiNiPO4: 5.1-5.2 V (literature, but CHGNet overestimates to ~7 V per the calibration note) — excluded family. 46	- LiMnPO4: 4.1 V. 47	- Fluorophosphates: LiVPO4F (4.2 V, tavorite), Li2MPO4F (M=Mn, Fe, Co, Ni — fluorophosphate). Li2NiPO4F has been reported as high voltage. Li2CoPO4F: ~4.9-5.0 V theoretical. Actually Li2CoPO4F has been studied with reported voltage ~5.0 V. 48	- LiMn1.5Ni0.5O4 (LNMO spinel): 4.7-4.8 V — below 4.95. 49	- LiNi0.5Mn1.5O4: 4.7 V. 50	- Cr-based spinels LiCrMnO4: ~4.8 V. 51	- Oxyfluorides: Li2MnO2F etc. 52	- Polyanions with F: Li2FePO4F etc. 53	- Tavorite LiVPO4F: 4.2 V. 54	- LiCoMnO4: ~5.0 V (spinel with Co). LiCoMnO4 reported ~5.0-5.2 V? Actually LiCoMnO4 has an average voltage around 5.0 V (disordered spinel). Literature: LiCoMnO4 voltage ~5.1 V vs Li (Mn3+/4+ ~4.1, Co3+/4+ ~5.3?). Hmm. 55	- LiNiVO4 (inverse spinel): ~4.8 V. 56	- Li2NiPO4F: predicted ~5.2-5.4 V? There are papers on Li2MPO4F family: Li2NiPO4F has been computed with high voltages (~5.3 V). 57	- Li2CoPO4F: ~5.1 V (computed). 58	- LiMn0.5Fe0.5PO4F... 59	- LiFeSO4F (tavorite): 3.6 V. 60	- LiCoSO4F: 5.0 V predicted?  61	- Li2MnSiO4: 4.2-4.5 V. 62	- LiMnBO3... 63	- Li2MnP2O7... 64	- Li3V2(PO4)3: 4.1 V (3.9/4.1/4.6 three plateaus; last one 4.6 V). 65	- LiVP2O7... 66	- Li2CoP2O7? 67	- LiCrP2O7: 4.8 V. 68	- Li2CrP2O7?  69	- LiCrF2(SO4)?  70	- Fluorosulfates: LiMSO4F (M = Fe: 3.9 V tavorite; Co: ~5.0 V?). LiCoSO4F predicted 5.0 V? Yes, there are reports of LiCoSO4F with ~4.9-5.0 V. 71	- Li2FeMn(SO4)3? 72	 73	Given the "true (QE-level or literature-consistent) voltage >= 4.95 V" requirement, good candidates with literature backing: 74	1. LiCoPO4: ~4.8 V — slightly below 4.95. 75	2. Li2CoPO4F: reported ~4.9-5.1 V. 76	3. LiCoMnO4 spinel: ~5.0 V reported. 77	4. LiNi0.5Mn1.5O4: 4.7 V — no. 78	5. Li2NiPO4F: ~5.2 V computed but it's Ni... The exclusion is about "Ni-containing olivines" overestimate family (CHGNet Ni-olivine overestimate). Li2NiPO4F is a fluorophosphate with Ni — is it in the overestimate family? The calibration note says Ni-containing olivines compute inflated high voltage in CHGNet. Li2NiPO4F is not an olivine, but CHGNet's overestimate is probably driven by Ni redox in certain coordination. Hmm, the true-voltage guard: "computed-only high values from the Ni-olivine overestimate family are rejected at adjudication regardless of (B)". So if I choose a Ni-rich composition whose computed voltage is high but whose true voltage is < 4.95, it's rejected. If true voltage is ≥ 4.95 and it's genuinely high, it might pass B'. But the guidance says "in-family high-volt extrapolation (Ni-richer variants, doping) lands on known/near-known computed points or the overestimate family and is not a path". 79	 80	So the recommended path: import chemistry from outside the cathode domain — fluorophosphates, oxyfluorides, other frameworks — and compute honestly.
